From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Beating the Stock Market?

On a lower tech note ...  .

My theory of stock market investing is based on the idea of efficient
markets. I assume the price contains all publicly available information,
and that therefore only an insider can make abnormal gains. But an insider,
in this context, is anyone with better-than-public information--where
information includes judgement.

For example... . When the Macintosh came out, I had been using a personal
computer (LNW80) for some years. It was clear to me both that the Mac
represented a major step forward and that the market, as judged both by
stock prices and news stories, had not recognized that. I accordingly
bought stock in Apple.

This approach does not require me to know much about the companies I invest
in. Suppose Apple's long run value depends mainly on four variables--how
competent the CEO is, what their capital structure is, ... , and how good a
product the Mac is. I assume that the market has already incorporated
public information on all four into the stock price. So if I am right in
believing that the market estimate of the fourth variable is low by (say)
$10, it follows that the estimate I would make of the value of the stock,
if I bothered to get all the public information on the other three, is the
market price plus $10.

Acting on this principle, I have bought stock in four companies over the
years: Apple, Microsoft, AOL, and (a few weeks ago) Psion. They have all
done very well for me. 

