Ditto for me. The Mac went on sale January 24, 1984, for around $2599
or somesuch. I went at looked at it and was impressed. (I also knew
Steve Jobs from the Homebrew Computer Club in the mid-70s.)

Instead of buying one, I instead bought 100 shares at $25 apiece,
which was a nice coincidence. Several years later I sold them for
about $10,000...enough to buy my Mac IIci a couple of times over.

(In between, I did buy a Mac Plus in 1986.)
 
My interest in "beating the stock market" is not in some
numerological, chartist-oriented "system," but in studying the rich
information flows in these complex markets.

In a system that demonstrably consists of many agents, each with their
own goals, time horizons, risk aversions, and wildly varying amounts
of information, I'm not sure the efficient market hypothesis is valid.
Maybe in equilibrium, whatever that is, but I suspect (but cannot
prove) that the stock market is nearly always not at equilibrium.

Fundamental analysis--of the sort David is talking about--has worked
well for me the past 15 years. Maybe now it's time to put a small
amount of money into a different appraoch.

Besides, it makes the study of markets, complexity, entropy, etc. so
much more interesting!

