This is "The Prediction Company." Doyne Farmer and others. I think one
of the recent "Complexity" books--probably the one by Mitchell
Waldrop--has a whole section on this development.

The "Chaos Cabal" described in several books, including the wonderful
little "Eudaemonic Pie" mentioned above, was located right here in
scenic Santa Cruz in the late 1970s and into the early 80s, when they
scattered (some to Los Alamos, some to the Santa Fe Institute, one of
them to join Steven Wolfram at Princeton, etc.). 

At the first Artificial Life Conference, held in 1987 at Los Alamos, I
got to meet many of them, who were drawn together again for this new
field. Lots of new stuff, including genetic algorithms, nonlinear
dynamics, and evolution in general. 

Anyway, The Prediction Company has had a few hiccups in getting
started with real money, but this supposedly is now happening. My
friend the hedge fund manager, about whom this thread got started, is
meeting (or already has met) with Farmer to discuss certain things of
mutual interest.

The recent debates about "determinism" and "free will" have left me
cold--which is my right and does not mean the topic is itself
boring--as I think these issues were disposed of as much as they can
be long ago.

Of much greater interest to me is the juicy--and experimentally
rich--area of "determinism in markets," that is, this whole area of
discovering or inducting algorithms for gaining a market edge.

_Not_ "predicting," just gaining a "thermodynamic edge," a way to in
the long run extract a surplus of energy (money, in mundane terms)
from a complex market.

If it is just _not_ possible to do this, as some have claimed in print
(the strong form of the efficient market hypothesis), then it will be
interesting to understand why this is so.

Frankly, I view the stock market as being much like markets in the
past. Thus, if a trader could decide that he could buy sugar in the
West Indies, ship it to the Colonies, sell it for tobacco to ship to
England, and so on (I'll ignore the 3rd leg in the cycle--slaves), and
make huge profits, "efficient market" be damned, then why not similar
opportunities in stocks and commodities?

